      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "To study the possibility that changes in high-sensitivity C-reactive protein (hs-CRP) may be a predictor of forced expiratory volume in 1 second (FEV1) decline over time in adult asthmatic subjects."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Subjects with adult-onset asthma with no smoking history underwent complete medical evaluation at Yoka Public Hospital in both 2005 and in 2008 (n = 26). We analyzed the correlation between hs-CRP levels and FEV1 changes over time, in relation to whether or not they received inhaled corticosteroid (ICS) therapy. We also measured the cardio-ankle vascular index (CAVI), intima-media-thickness (IMT), and fraction of exhaled nitric oxide (FeNO) of hospital staff members who acted as non ICS asthmatic subjects (n = 11), and also performed multiple regression analysis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In the non-ICS asthmatic subjects group (n = 19), there was a significant correlation between log (hs-CRP) levels and FEV1 changes (R = -0.734, P < 0.001). After controlling for age, body mass index, CAVI, IMT, and FeNO, hs-CRP was found to be an independent risk factor."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Hs-CRP levels are a predictor of FEV1 decline over time in adult-onset asthmatic patients with no smoking history, who are not receiving ICS therapy."
        }
      ]},
